  "slug": "omics-integration-multi-2531",
  "summary": "Recent papers pair spatial statistics, cross-omics translation and mass spectrometry imaging to resolve tissue-level biology.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is joint use of heterogeneous measurement layers within a single computational framework, with spatial and microenvironmental context serving as the organizing element. The senescence preprint applies Ripley's functions and functional data analysis, with senescence-signature scoring, to quantify senescent cells spatially. NicheTrans uses a spatially aware Transformer to translate between omics modalities conditioned on cellular microenvironment. The lung study coregisters mass spectrometry with nonlinear optical imaging through a hierarchical registration network to map metabolic changes across scales. The Cell preview of Li et al. integrates clinical, multi-omics and organ signatures into multi-layer clocks of biological aging. The Alzheimer's disease meta-program screen for drug repurposing is described only briefly in its summary. What is new relative to prior work is the use of spatial and organ-level context as the integrating axis across modalities, rather than fusion of sequence, expression and simulated readouts alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-09"
}
